# Rank 1649 / CVE-2016-10522: rails_admin's engine controllers inherited
# a runtime-configured parent controller whose default, since 53eef4fe
# ("Change parent controller to ActionController::Base for out-of-box
# support of Rails 5 API mode"), is '::ActionController::Base' -- a base
# that carries no forgery protection of its own -- so every
# state-changing admin endpoint (create, edit, delete, bulk destroy)
# accepted cross-site requests with no CSRF token (CWE-352; reported by
# SourceClear). The engine controller in app/controllers/rails_admin/
# application_controller.rb declares `class ApplicationController <
# Config.parent_controller.constantize` with before_action
# authentication and authorization filters and no protect_from_forgery
# of its own; the default that actually decides the protection,
# `@parent_controller = '::ActionController::Base'`, sits in
# lib/rails_admin/config.rb:290. The fixing commit b13e879eb9
# ("[Security] Fixes CSRF vulnerability, introduced by
# 53eef4fe2ec0953381f4e3197c885adc0423dd49", +2 lines of code): the
# engine's ApplicationController now declares
# `protect_from_forgery with: :exception` itself, so protection no
# longer depends on the configured parent.
#
# ATTENTION, class-level guard attribution, held on recheck. Re-measured
# at the span tip (3 x --cache off per revision, timeout 180, --max-ram
# 6GB): the tree reports 50 findings at the fix parent and 50 at the
# fix, identical sets by fingerprint diff (0 added, 0 removed, all three
# runs each side), all unrelated (INJ-004 on the asset javascript,
# RF-002 redirects, AUTH-002); the full findings view plus -flags with
# gives 117 review flags at the parent and 119 at the fix, the delta
# being exactly the fix's own core.CsrfProtection label on
# application_controller.rb:14 and an ObjectLookupByUserInput on the
# integration spec the fix commit adds. No rule shipped since the first
# pass fires on the vulnerable revision.
#
# The first pass's diagnosis is half corrected by the re-measurement:
# the taint route is not blocked. VYQL-INJ-012 already reports the
# mass-assignment path lib/rails_admin/config/actions/edit.rb:27 ->
# lib/rails_admin/adapters/active_record/abstract_object.rb:19, so
# `@object.set_attributes(params[@abstract_model.param_key])` resolves
# through the wrapper into `object.assign_attributes(attributes)` and
# the params reach the write. What is missing is narrower: a
# code.StateChangingOpSink binding for the documented Rails
# mass-assignment API, and a guard relation that can see the class-level
# forgery declaration. Measured with a throwaway binding emitting
# code.StateChangingOpSink at args[0] of assign_attributes/
# update_attributes/set_attributes: VYQL-RF-003 fires four times at the
# parent AND four times, same fingerprints, at the fix (new.rb:23->25,
# new.rb:38, and edit.rb:27 twice, once via abstract_object.rb:19) --
# zero discrimination, and four findings sitting on the remediated
# revision. The suppression clause is why: `unless endpoint coveredBy
# core.CsrfProtection` only connects a guard in the same function
# (sameFunctionContextGuarded, internal/engine/engine.go:1216, requires
# an identical functionScopeKey, and a class-body macro carries no fn
# key at all) or one that dominates the sink in one function's CFG. A
# class-body protect_from_forgery never covers a method body, and the
# real shape is one step wider still -- the declaration sits in the
# engine's ApplicationController while the write runs in an action proc
# registered from lib/rails_admin/config/actions/edit.rb.
#
# The minimal pair at the bottom isolates exactly that construct: the
# same guarded controller with the write in a method reports VYQL-RF-003
# under the probe while moving the guard call into the method suppresses
# it, one line apart. Adding the relation is not a localised correction
# -- it is a new coverage route (a control emitted at class scope
# covers the class's methods and subclasses), shared by every language
# that emits a class-level control with `covers endpoint` (dart, python,
# go, scala, groovy, elixir, lua, csharp, php, ruby), and it needs
# argument awareness to stay sound: Rails' `only:`/`except:`/`with:`
# options and `skip_before_action :verify_authenticity_token` opt-outs
# are the distinction the shipped RailsBasicAuthWeakCsrfStrategy binding
# already keys on, and the parent here is resolved at runtime
# (`Config.parent_controller.constantize`), which no frontend resolves.
#
# The presence route stays unshippable for the same reason as the first
# pass found: a class-scope binding on the dynamic-superclass idiom
# (`classBase contains "constantize"` with no protect_from_forgery in
# the class body) does fire 1 -> 0 on this repository, but it keys on
# the idiom rather than the weakness -- the documented safe
# configuration (`config.parent_controller = '::ApplicationController'`)
# and Devise's identical `parent_controller.constantize` idiom leave the
# controller file textually identical while inheriting protection from
# the host application's ApplicationController, because the deciding
# value lives in another file (lib/rails_admin/config.rb:290 against the
# application's initializer). Across the pool clones only this
# repository carries the idiom at all, so the false-positive rate of the
# idiom binding cannot even be measured on the corpus. No pool
# correction: b13e879eb9 is the real fix.
#
# The spec pins five forms, all rejecting VYQL-RF-003 today: the engine
# controller as shipped and as fixed, the edit action's write proc, and
# the guarded and unguarded canonical controller. When the
# state-changing sink vocabulary and the class-level guard relation land
# together, the unguarded canonical form and the two real-code forms
# must flip to expect while the guarded form and the fixed engine
# controller must stay rejected.

test "rank1649: the engine controller inherits the configured parent with no forgery declaration of its own"
  lang ruby
  file app/controllers/rails_admin/application_controller.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  code
  ```
  require 'rails_admin/abstract_model'

  module RailsAdmin
    class ApplicationController < Config.parent_controller.constantize
      before_action :_authenticate!
      before_action :_authorize!
      before_action :_audit!

      helper_method :_current_user, :_get_plugin_name

      attr_reader :object, :model_config, :abstract_model, :authorization_adapter

      def get_model
        @model_name = to_model_name(params[:model_name])
        raise(RailsAdmin::ModelNotFound) unless (@abstract_model = RailsAdmin::AbstractModel.new(@model_name))
        raise(RailsAdmin::ModelNotFound) if (@model_config = @abstract_model.config).excluded?
        @properties = @abstract_model.properties
      end

      def get_object
        raise(RailsAdmin::ObjectNotFound) unless (@object = @abstract_model.get(params[:id]))
      end

      def to_model_name(param)
        param.split('~').collect(&:camelize).join('::')
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1649: the fixed revision declares forgery protection in the engine controller itself"
  lang ruby
  file app/controllers/rails_admin/application_controller.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  code
  ```
  require 'rails_admin/abstract_model'

  module RailsAdmin
    class ApplicationController < Config.parent_controller.constantize
      protect_from_forgery with: :exception

      before_action :_authenticate!
      before_action :_authorize!
      before_action :_audit!

      helper_method :_current_user, :_get_plugin_name

      attr_reader :object, :model_config, :abstract_model, :authorization_adapter

      def get_model
        @model_name = to_model_name(params[:model_name])
        raise(RailsAdmin::ModelNotFound) unless (@abstract_model = RailsAdmin::AbstractModel.new(@model_name))
        raise(RailsAdmin::ModelNotFound) if (@model_config = @abstract_model.config).excluded?
        @properties = @abstract_model.properties
      end

      def get_object
        raise(RailsAdmin::ObjectNotFound) unless (@object = @abstract_model.get(params[:id]))
      end

      def to_model_name(param)
        param.split('~').collect(&:camelize).join('::')
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1649: the edit action applies request parameters to the object"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rails_admin/config/actions/edit.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  code
  ```
  module RailsAdmin
    module Config
      module Actions
        class Edit < RailsAdmin::Config::Actions::Base
          register_instance_option :http_methods do
            [:get, :put]
          end

          register_instance_option :controller do
            proc do
              if request.get?
                respond_to do |format|
                  format.html { render @action.template_name }
                end
              elsif request.put?
                sanitize_params_for!(request.xhr? ? :modal : :update)

                @object.set_attributes(params[@abstract_model.param_key])
                @authorization_adapter && @authorization_adapter.attributes_for(:update, @abstract_model).each do |name, value|
                  @object.send("#{name}=", value)
                end
                changes = @object.changes
                if @object.save
                  @auditing_adapter && @auditing_adapter.update_object(@object, @abstract_model, _current_user, changes)
                  respond_to do |format|
                    format.html { redirect_to_on_success }
                  end
                else
                  handle_save_error :edit
                end
              end
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1649: a class-body forgery declaration must cover the action that writes"
  lang ruby
  file app/controllers/posts_controller.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  code
  ```
  class PostsController < ApplicationController
    protect_from_forgery with: :exception

    def create
      @post = Post.new
      @post.assign_attributes(params[:post])
      @post.save
    end
  end
  ```

